1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,952
Tilt: numbers change. Touch: a position appears. Press: the display changes. Let's

2
00:00:06,952 --> 00:00:10,020
discover how the board senses all that.

3
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:16,460
The accelerometer feels pushes and tilts in three directions: X, Y and Z.

4
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:25,065
The chip talks to it using I2C. Two wires: one carries a clock beat, one carries

5
00:00:25,065 --> 00:00:25,540
data.

6
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:30,945
Every device on the street has an address, its house number. Inside are numbered

7
00:00:30,945 --> 00:00:32,600
mailboxes called registers.

8
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:39,820
Our sensor may live at one of four addresses, so the chip knocks on each. A reply to

9
00:00:39,820 --> 00:00:42,700
a knock is called an acknowledgment.

10
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:49,110
Then it reads the 'who are you' mailbox, number zero D. The dashboard hopes to hear

11
00:00:49,110 --> 00:00:49,680
C seven.

12
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:56,900
The smaller demo also accepts one A, a different sensor used on some boards.

13
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:00,460
Each direction arrives as two bytes.

14
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:06,349
The chip glues them together, drops two empty bits at the bottom while keeping the

15
00:01:06,349 --> 00:01:11,474
plus or minus sign, and scales the result into milli-g: thousandths of Earth's

16
00:01:11,474 --> 00:01:12,000
gravity.

17
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:17,460
A board lying still and flat. Which direction should read about a thousand?

18
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:22,960
Your turn. Pause the video.

19
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:28,240
Z, pointing up or down. The sign shows which way up it is.

20
00:01:29,240 --> 00:01:35,529
The touch pads are just copper. Your finger changes how much electric charge a pad

21
00:01:35,529 --> 00:01:40,360
can hold, called capacitance. The chip measures it as a number.

22
00:01:40,860 --> 00:01:46,628
At startup it records each pad with no finger: the baseline. So keep your hands off

23
00:01:46,628 --> 00:01:47,740
while it starts.

24
00:01:48,740 --> 00:01:54,127
Subtract the baselines. If both changes together are tiny, nobody is touching.

25
00:01:54,127 --> 00:01:56,820
Otherwise, find the second pad's share.

26
00:01:57,320 --> 00:02:02,153
Changes of twenty and eighty give a position of eighty. Near zero means the first

27
00:02:02,153 --> 00:02:04,360
pad; near a hundred means the second.

28
00:02:05,360 --> 00:02:11,326
When should the board report? A hardware timer called PIT rings at a steady rate and

29
00:02:11,326 --> 00:02:12,320
raises a flag.

30
00:02:12,820 --> 00:02:18,923
The main loop sees the flag, reads the sensors and prints. The button has its own

31
00:02:18,923 --> 00:02:19,300
flag.

32
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:23,880
The loop checks both, because both can be up together.

33
00:02:24,880 --> 00:02:27,280
Reading sensors and printing take time.

34
00:02:27,780 --> 00:02:33,344
If ticks arrive faster than the work finishes, a flag only says 'work is waiting',

35
00:02:33,344 --> 00:02:35,380
not how many ticks you missed.

36
00:02:35,880 --> 00:02:39,320
At five updates per second, how long between ticks?

37
00:02:39,820 --> 00:02:44,820
Your turn. Pause the video.

38
00:02:44,820 --> 00:02:48,580
Two hundred thousand microseconds, a fifth of a second.

39
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:56,300
Addresses for tilt, baselines for touch, and a metronome for timing. Now let's build

40
00:02:56,300 --> 00:02:56,700
them.

